# Constant folding: recursion through "all children"
#
# The rule a compiler writer wants is "an expression is constant if it is a
# literal, or it is composite and *every* child is constant". Datalog has no
# `forall`, so you name the counterexample and negate it: an expression is
# constant when it has no non-constant child.
#
# That puts `constant` in a cycle with itself through two negations. One
# negation in a cycle has no fixed point and is rejected. Two cancel: the
# operator on `constant` is monotone again, and only the helper in the middle
# is anti-monotone. The `^` sigil says which side that is, so the evaluator
# knows to start it at ⊤ and rebuild it each round while `constant` grows.
#
# Read `not has_nonconstant_child^(E)` as "no child of E fails to be constant",
# and the `^` as the marker that this negation is deliberately inside a cycle.
# Without the sigil the program is rejected, exactly as it was before this
# feature existed.

input predicate literal(e: string).
input predicate composite(e: string).
input predicate child(parent: string, kid: string).

constant(E) :- literal(E).
constant(E) :- composite(E), not has_nonconstant_child^(E).

has_nonconstant_child^(E) :- child(E, C), not constant(C).

?- constant(E).

# The expressions are two arithmetic trees sharing a subterm: `(1 + 2) * x` and
# `(1 + 2) * 4`. `x` is a variable, so it is neither a literal nor composite and
# no rule can ever derive it constant. That makes `mul_x` non-constant while
# `add` and `mul_4` fold.
#
# Evaluation takes one round per level of the tree. Round 1 has the helper at ⊤,
# so nothing but the literals is constant; round 2 adds `add`; round 3 adds
# `mul_4` and reaches the fixed point.

output predicate not_constant(E) :- composite(E), not constant(E).

# Anything with a constant child that is itself not constant is where folding
# stops: the boundary a real optimiser would rewrite.
output predicate fold_boundary(Parent, Kid) :-
  child(Parent, Kid), constant(Kid), not constant(Parent).
